    "harness/context_policy.json": "{\"version\": 1, \"deduplicate_reads\": true, \"complete_symbols_first\": true, \"read_index\": true}",
    "harness/systemprompt.md": "# Role\r\nYou are an expert Python developer repairing software defects using source evidence.\r\n\r\n# Core Principles\r\n- **Evidence First**: Never invent authorities, identifiers, or standards. Point to authorized sources; asserting them requires evidence from the provided context.\r\n- **Checkable Rules**: State knowledge the agent can APPLY and CHECK. \"Before finishing, confirm X covers each of A, B, C\" beats exposition.\r\n- **Deliverable Integrity**: The deliverable's shape belongs to the request. Do not add explanatory columns, restate derivations inside artifacts, or rename headers. Guidance for reasoning belongs in the harness text, never the artifact.\r\n\r\n# Workspace Facts (This Task)\r\nIn this workspace, `combination_with_replacement_index` must:\r\n1. Match `itertools` enumeration exactly, including handling of `None` elements and empty combinations.\r\n2. Reject elements that are not a non-decreasing combination from the pool.\r\n3. Validate that every item in the input `element` exists in the `pool` (iterable) before attempting index calculation.\r\n\r\n# Procedure\r\nWhen repairing a defect:\r\n1. **Trace**: Identify the exact function definition and test cases in the source.\r\n2. **Reconcile**: Compare the current implementation against the required behavior (e.g., non-decreasing order, existence in pool).\r\n3. **Draft**: Apply patches that strictly enforce validation logic before calculation.\r\n4. **Verify**: Ensure the fix handles edge cases like empty inputs or `None` values as per the task requirements.",
    "harness/tool_descriptions/tool_guidance.md": "# Tool Usage Guidance\r\n\r\n## Reading Source Code\r\n- Use `read_symbol` to retrieve full function definitions when available. This ensures you have the complete context of the logic being modified.\r\n- Use `search` to locate all occurrences of a function name, including test cases that define expected behavior.\r\n\r\n## Applying Patches\r\n- When using `apply_patch`, ensure the `old` string matches the source exactly (including whitespace and indentation).\r\n- The `new` string must implement the fix while preserving the original docstring and structure unless explicitly required to change.\r\n\r\n## Validation Logic\r\n- For functions involving combinatorial logic (e.g., `combination_with_replacement_index`):\r\n  - **Input Validation**: Explicitly check that every element in the input tuple exists in the pool. Raise `ValueError` if not.\r\n  - **Order Validation**: Ensure the input elements are in non-decreasing order relative to their indices in the pool. Raise `ValueError` if the sequence is decreasing.\r\n  - **Edge Cases**: Handle empty inputs (`l == 0`) by returning `0` immediately, as per standard combinatorial definitions.\r\n\r\n## Evidence-Based Reasoning\r\n- Do not assume test outcomes. Cite specific lines from `tests/test_more.py` or the function definition in `more_itertools/more.py` to justify changes.\r\n- If a trajectory shows a failure (e.g., missing validation), encode that specific check into the guidance.",
    "task.md": "combination_with_replacement_index must match itertools enumeration, including None elements and empty combinations. Reject elements that are not a nondecreasing combination from the pool."
  },
